In <1993Feb4.150240.10975@mnemosyne.cs.du.edu> sjhoward@nyx.cs.du.edu (StinkFoot) writes:

>OK, silly me, why does the whole project have to be in one
>segment? What keeps one from dumping init routines, making 
>CODE resources purgable for functions like help, etc...

Just the simplest way to do it. The MPW version splits each
file in its own segment, except "some" files, and we have a
very clever unloading cheme called by the growzone proc,
gratuitously stolen from modern garbage-collection schemes.

>I can understand the need for needing a larger than 32K data structure for
>the Dungeon, but why not spilt it up into segments for The Valley,
>the Mall, the Mines, etc....

Naah, it's already split up in all those .lev files, and there's
only one level in memory at one time. The larga data comes from
all the embedded strings (if you don't have 'em in the CODE), from
all objects and monsters, and from the vision system.

